<article class="env-lemma" id="0BUX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BUX">Lemma <span data-tag="0BUX">47.11.4</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Noetherian local ring. If $M$ is a finite Cohen-Macaulay $R$-module and $I \subset R$ a nontrivial ideal. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{depth}_ I(M) = \dim (\text{Supp}(M)) - \dim (\text{Supp}(M/IM)).  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will prove this by induction on $\text{depth}_ I(M)$. 
    <p>
      If $\text{depth}_ I(M) = 0$, then $I$ is contained in one of the associated primes $\mathfrak p$ of $M$ (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00LL" data-tag="00LL">10.63.18</a>). Then $\mathfrak p \in \text{Supp}(M/IM)$, hence $\dim (\text{Supp}(M/IM)) \geq \dim (R/\mathfrak p) = \dim (\text{Supp}(M))$ where equality holds by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BUS" data-tag="0BUS">10.103.7</a>. Thus the lemma holds in this case. 
    <p>
      If $\text{depth}_ I(M) > 0$, we pick $x \in I$ which is a nonzerodivisor on $M$. Note that $(M/xM)/I(M/xM) = M/IM$. On the other hand we have $\text{depth}_ I(M/xM) = \text{depth}_ I(M) - 1$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0BUW" data-tag="0BUW">47.11.3</a> and $\dim (\text{Supp}(M/xM)) = \dim (\text{Supp}(M)) - 1$ by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0B52" data-tag="0B52">10.63.10</a>. Thus the result by induction hypothesis. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>